            "text": "Gives the inverse of a square matrix: the matrix that undoes it.\n\nIf a matrix turns, stretches or shears things, its inverse turns, shrinks or un-shears them back. Multiplying a matrix by its inverse gives the identity matrix - 1s down the diagonal, 0s everywhere else - which changes nothing. That is the definition, and the demo checks it live.\n\nReach for it when you need the undoing matrix itself: to map screen positions back into a camera's frame, to reverse a calibration, to keep both directions of a transformation. To answer one question of the form \"which input gives this output?\", np.linalg.solve is faster and more accurate than inverting and multiplying.\n\nNOT EVERY MATRIX HAS ONE:\nA matrix whose determinant is 0 squashes space flat, and that cannot be undone. Such a matrix is called singular; np.linalg.inv sends nothing for it and the displays keep the last good answer. A matrix that is NEARLY singular does invert, but into huge numbers - small errors in the input become large errors in the output.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.linalg.inv\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nA square matrix (a list of lists is turned into an array). Each one that arrives is inverted and sent. A matrix that is not square, a 1-D list, or a singular matrix sends nothing (the reason is printed only in verbose mode). A stack of square matrices is inverted one by one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ninverse matrix:\nThe inverse, the same shape as the input, as floats.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nDrag the numbers of the matrix. Its rows start [2 1] and [1 1], whose inverse is [1 -1] and [-1 2]. The third picture is np.matmul of the matrix and its inverse: the identity, blue 1s on the diagonal, pale 0s off it - and it stays the identity however you change the matrix. Make the rows parallel (bottom row 2 1) and the matrix is singular: no inverse is sent, the middle picture keeps the last good one, and the product of the new matrix with that old inverse is no longer the identity - the sign that something went wrong.\nThe t array array sends to np.linalg.inv first (it fires right to left), so the inverse is waiting in np.matmul's second inlet when the matrix arrives at its first.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.solve answers A x = b without forming the inverse. np.linalg.det says whether an inverse exists (it must not be 0). np.matmul applies a matrix. np.transpose is the inverse of a pure rotation matrix, and much cheaper."
        },
